module gunbc.whole_corpus_compile_admission

import std.types { Int, String, NonEmptyStr, Bool }
import std.measure { ByteSize, byte_size, byte_size_count }
import std.disposition { Disposition, Terminal, Scaffold, SingleAuthority }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.roadmap_model { RoadmapNodeId }
import gunbc.seed_growth { SeedGrowthJustification }
import gunbc.host_budget_source {
  HostBudgetResolution,
  HostBudgetResolved,
  HostBudgetDeclaredUnverified,
  HostBudgetUnreadable,
  HostBudgetSource,
  HostBudgetObserved,
  host_budget_source_label,
}

data whole_corpus_compile_admission_authority_disposition: Disposition = Terminal {
  reason: "Single authority for the arm-time admission of a WHOLE-CORPUS compile against the host memory budget gunbc.host_budget_source already models. The budget half and the source half were both modeled; the JOIN between them and the decision to start the run was not, which is the defect this module closes. Its sibling for the floor walk is gunbc.runner_slot_allocation gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget, enforced by claim_executor at arm time as a fail-fast refusal on a provably doomed walk; nothing played that role for the compile instrument, and this module is that role, derived the same way from a measured demand figure."
}

// THE OBSERVATION EXISTED, WAS CORRECT, AND WAS JOINED TO NOTHING — which is why the fix is a join
// and not a bigger machine. v1_compiler.cli_run typed_module_cache_cap derives an entry cap from
// the host budget once per index and, when the budget source is degraded, prints [floor-drain]
// degraded_budget_source: cap=<n> source=<label>. That line is a write-only eprintln: no consumer
// reads it. AND THE TENSE OF THE NEXT CLAUSE IS LOAD-BEARING, because this module names the thing
// that falsifies it. BEFORE THE ADMISSION JOIN LANDED, no admission decision read that budget and
// the process then started a whole-corpus resolve whose resident set exceeded what the budget could
// hold -- that is the observation this note exists to record. NOW, v1_compiler.cli_run
// compile_emission reads the same budget authority, invokes whole_corpus_compile_admission, and can
// return NotExecuted BEFORE indexing. Stated in the present tense, this sentence said `no admission
// reads it` in the same module that declares the live admission, so one module asserted a fact and
// its own refutation; corrected 2026-08-28, found by an external reader against the current head
// rather than by anything that re-derives it.
//
// A run that knows its budget and starts a job it cannot hold is not resource-starved, it is
// UN-JOINED. Resource-starved is answered by a larger machine; un-joined produces the identical
// failure on the larger machine one corpus-growth later. DESIGN section 5 names the failure shape
// exactly: the deficit's frequency is zero by construction, so it never ranks for fixing, and the
// cost arrives as a dead process instead of a diagnostic.
//
// HOW IT ARRIVES, and why it is worse than a crash: the kill is SIGKILL. There is no diagnostic, no
// partial-result marker, and a harness that greps the captured output for a count reads ZERO — a
// fabricated zero indistinguishable from a real one. The session that found this had a harness
// printing only the grep result and would have published 'the corpus carries 0 rows of class X';
// echoing the exit status explicitly is the only thing that stopped it. Every corpus-wide count
// taken over a run whose exit status was not recorded is unattributed for the same reason, and that
// is a standing reading rule rather than a fact about one night.

data whole_corpus_compile_observed_kill_receipt_note: String = "MEASURED, TWICE, ON THE BUILDBUDDY REMOTE-EXECUTION RUNNER (session stern-heron-695, 2026-08-19/20). The instrument is the one v1.compiler.04_infer method_existence_wall_note names as the whole-corpus instrument — gunbc compile --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --target dag — and which that row contrasts against a narrow --entry run that reported clean while twelve real sites sat outside its closure. Invocations a39713da-8cfb-415d-a8f6-1e0ef150d075 (at 517fdac4dd) and 13cf8d2e-173a-42d2-9a56-101bb3332740 (at 0456098671) both ended 'Killed' with EXIT=137. The BUILD succeeded in about four minutes each time; the COMPILE died. Reproducible, not a flake.\n\nTHE CAP LINE IS THE PREDICTION, AND ITS UNITS ARE ENTRIES, NOT BYTES. The second run printed, immediately before dying: [floor-drain] degraded_budget_source: cap=1675 source=/proc/meminfo MemAvailable (no private cgroup memory.max/memory.high found). 1675 is a MODULE-ENTRY count, not a megabyte figure — the reporting session read it as '1675 MB' and derived from that a '~1.6 GiB runner', which is corrected here rather than inherited. cli_run derives cap as budget_bytes / TYPED_MODULE_BYTES_PER_ENTRY_ESTIMATE (3 MiB) clamped to [100, 4000], and 1675 sits strictly inside that clamp, so the reading INVERTS EXACTLY: MemAvailable on that runner was in [5269094400, 5272240128) bytes — about 4.9 GiB, not 1.6. THE CAPACITY INVERSION SURVIVES THE CORRECTION and is the operator-facing half: the session container carries a 31 GiB cgroup cap while the remote-execution runner answered with under 5 GiB, so the smaller machine is the one that ran it. WHAT IS MEASURED AND WHAT IS NOT, separated 2026-08-28 because this clause asserted a cause it never established. MEASURED: both runs occurred on BuildBuddy, the runner exposed about 4.9 GiB, both ended by SIGKILL. NOT ESTABLISHED: why they went there. The clause read 'remote is the DEFAULT route, so a memory-bound job is sent to the small machine automatically' — that default is a fact about the cargo shim, which does not route a bare `gunbc compile` binary, and no invocation record here says how these two were dispatched. The causal story is WITHDRAWN rather than repaired, because the receipts ground the resource failure without it.\n\nWHY THE CORRECTION MAKES THE JOIN MORE URGENT RATHER THAN LESS. THE MAGNITUDE THIS CLAUSE ONCE CARRIED WAS A PROPERTY OF THE SUPERSEDED PROXY AND IS RESTATED AS HISTORY. It read that the deficit is of order one to two gibibytes -- true against the 7 GiB proxy and false now: the BuildBuddy lower bound is about 4.9 GiB, this session's highest refusing peak is 12.41 GiB and the declared floor is 16 GiB (adopted from the highest measured COMPLETING peak, 15.14 GiB), so the gaps are about 7.5 and 11.1 GiB. The DURABLE conclusion is the one that never depended on the magnitude: a budget observed and joined to nothing produces the identical failure on a larger machine one corpus-growth later, so the fix is the join and not the requisition. What is retired is the argument FROM SMALLNESS -- that a deficit that small is exactly the one a bigger machine appears to fix -- because the deficit is no longer small. Corrected 2026-08-28 alongside the tense repair above, and by the same reader: both sentences were true when written and were falsified by this PR's own measurement, which is the failure mode of a number living in prose beside the thing that moves it. An un-joined instrument that gets a bigger machine survives to fail again, silently, on the next corpus growth. That is the whole argument for landing a refusal rather than a requisition."

data whole_corpus_compile_measured_demand_note: String = "WHERE THE THRESHOLD COMES FROM, AND WHY IT IS NOT A NUMBER FROM THIS TREE (DESIGN section 5 test-oracle rule: a merge-blocking literal must be grounded in a controlled fixture, an external or versioned authority, or a policy budget — and a measurement copied from the current tree is not an oracle).\n\nTHE PROXY IS RETIRED. This figure was 7 GiB, derived from two 2026-07-21 CI receipts that measured the compile-clean whole-tree pass on the FLOOR route rather than gunbc compile's own emit leg, and that note declared its own re-measure trigger: a dated uncensored whole-tree peak taken on the compile route itself. THAT TRIGGER HAS FIRED and this row is rewritten rather than amended, because every load-bearing sentence of the proxy version was a statement about a substitute that is no longer needed.\n\nTHE RECEIPT. Session clever-tern-899, 2026-08-28, on srv1 (502 GiB total, 334 GiB available at arm time, aarch64), against a clean tree staged at 91c05c1b344d29f97a363eaff34843177d552a99 with a binary BUILT FROM THAT SAME SHA. Both targets were measured, since the admission arm does not discriminate on target:\n\n  gunbc compile --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --target dag   EXIT=1  peak 13008052 kB  wall 25:04\n  gunbc compile --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --target rust  EXIT=1  peak 13005964 kB  wall 27:44\n\nEXIT STATUS AND PEAK WERE CAPTURED TOGETHER, per whole_corpus_compile_unjoined_observation_note: a SIGKILL prints nothing and reads as a clean zero, so a peak without an exit status is unattributed. Both runs COMPLETED and refused on diagnostics; neither was killed. A POSITIVE CONTROL ran first — the scoped compile of src/v2/std/node.dag returned EXIT=0 with a file emitted — so the harness produces both outcomes and a refusal is a finding rather than a broken rig.\n\nUNCENSORED IN THE SENSE gunbc.runner_slot_allocation gunbc_floor_measured_peak_is_uncensored DEMANDS: the host had 271 GiB available at exit against a 12.4 GiB peak, so nothing throttled either run and neither peak is an armed line being mistaken for demand.\n\nTHE GRAIN IS THE RECEIPT'S GRAIN, ROUNDED IN THE FAIL-CLOSED DIRECTION -- a rule stated here, applied below to whichever reading is highest. The higher reading in THIS paragraph is 13008052 kB = 12.41 GiB, which rounds up to 13 GiB; that was the declared figure until the completing-run adoption further down superseded it, and it is kept here in the past tense because the re-measure trigger is defined by comparison against these readings. Rounding UP is what makes it fail-closed: for a DEMAND figure rounding up refuses the marginal case and rounding down admits it. The two targets agree to within 0.02%, which is why one figure serves both rather than each target carrying its own.\n\nWHAT THIS CORRECTS, STATED AS A FAIL-OPEN RATHER THAN AS STALENESS. 7 GiB was declared a LOWER bound and was 43.6% BELOW the measured peak — 7 GiB against 12.41 GiB, or 56.4% of it — so the arm ADMITTED hosts it would then be killed on. (This read '64% under' until 2026-08-28. That figure was computed as (11.5-7)/7 against the CONFOUNDED 11.5 GiB peak this change retracts, expressed as a percentage ABOVE the old declaration, then carried across and relabelled 'under' when the peak was corrected to 12.41 — wrong denominator and a withdrawn measurement, in one number. The percentage now names its denominator, which is the only form a reader can check.) A refusal that cannot fire for the population needing it is not conservative; it is the shape DESIGN section 5 names, where the deficit's frequency is zero by construction. THE SAME CLASS WAS THEN FOUND ONE BAND NARROWER IN THIS ROW'S OWN 13 GiB CORRECTION, by review 57202, and is addressed at the adoption paragraph below; a sentence here once read that the 15 GiB CI runner slot still admits with about 21% headroom, which the adoption falsifies -- the slot is now refused, deliberately.\n\nEVERY PEAK ABOVE IS A REFUSING RUN'S PEAK, AND THAT IS A BOUND RATHER THAN THE DEMAND. Both runs exited 1 on diagnostics, so neither paid for the stages after the gate it stopped at. A third run was taken to close that gap and DID NOT CLOSE IT (session clever-tern-899, srv1, the same binary and staged tree, --target rust with the three parameter-default sites patched to literal defaults): EXIT=1, peak 12986524 kB, wall 25:05, zero files emitted. Clearing the parameter-default gate revealed EIGHT anonymous-record refusals standing behind it -- ambiguous anonymous record literal shapes in dag/test/claim/access_validation_test.dag (5), dag/extdeps/git/versioning.dag, dag/extdeps/realization/reconcile_in_process.dag and dag/test/claim/cache_retention_axes_witness_test.dag -- reproducing on the live corpus exactly the three-then-eight prefix that gunbc.emit_stage_blocking_population_census already records. So the rust route's blocking population is still not bounded, and no completing whole-corpus compile has been measured by this session.\n\nWHAT THE THIRD RUN DID ESTABLISH, because a failed attempt at one question can answer another: the peak did not move. 12986524 kB against 13005964 kB is 0.15% LOWER while stopping at a strictly later gate, so the resident set is dominated by the shared front half -- frontend, normalize, reconcile -- and not by emission. A refusing peak is therefore a STABLE floor across gates rather than a figure that creeps toward the true demand as gates clear, which means the remaining gap to a completing run cannot be estimated from these three readings at all.\n\nWHAT THE SUBJECT ACTUALLY WAS, AND IT IS NARROWER THAN THE WORD WHOLE-CORPUS SUGGESTS. All three runs report the same subject line: `resolved 3109 sources (primary-root population root=dag), 1061 indexed modules in the name census only`. The CLI treats the FIRST --source-root as the PRIMARY population and later roots as DEPENDENCY POOLS, so `--source-root dag --source-root src/v2` makes dag's 3109 sources the subject and leaves src/v2's 1061 modules INDEXED BUT NOT COMPILED AS SUBJECT. This figure is therefore the demand of a dag-primary compile, not of the corpus, and src/v2 -- the v2 compiler itself, which is what self-host is about -- was never the subject of any run behind it.\n\nTHAT IS A THIRD INDEPENDENT REASON THE FIGURE IS A LOWER BOUND, and it is worth separating from the other two because they fail differently. The runs refused before codegen (bound: phases). The dag route's emission is not the rust route's (bound: route). And the subject excluded 1061 modules (bound: POPULATION). A reader who closes any one of these has not closed the others. A compile taking src/v2 as its primary root is a DIFFERENT SUBJECT with its own demand, unmeasured here, and DESIGN already records that such a run carries twenty hard diagnostics at its emit refusal -- so it is reachable and simply has not been priced.\n\nFound 2026-08-28 by an external reader asking what the S2 subject must bind, not by anything in this repository that re-derives it. The word `whole-corpus` in this module's own name is what made the gap invisible: it names an ambition rather than the argv that was run.\n\nWHICH PHASES THE PEAK COVERS, READ FROM THE THREE RUNS' OWN PHASE LINES RATHER THAN INFERRED. All three complete compile.frontend (43-48s), compile.normalize (3s), compile.reconcile (20-22 MINUTES -- the dominant cost by an order of magnitude) and compile.analyses (2-3s). TWO OF THEM ALSO COMPLETE compile.emit, in 2 minutes each: both rust-route runs ran the emit stage to return and refused on diagnostics AFTER it, while the dag-route run did not reach that line. So the emit stage's VALIDATION passes are inside these peaks and contribute nothing measurable to them -- the run WITH a completed emit peaked LOWER than the one without. What is NOT inside them is emit's CODE GENERATION, which no run here reached: DESIGN records emit with both gates cleared running five hours without returning, so codegen's own demand is unmeasured and this figure says nothing about it.\n\nTHAT IS WHY THE CROSS-GATE AGREEMENT IS EVIDENCE AND NOT A COINCIDENCE, a reading warm-hawk-909 supplied and these phase lines confirm. The standing worry about any demand figure is that it came from a run that stopped early and the true demand is unbounded above. Three runs stopping at THREE DIFFERENT POINTS -- before emit, after emit's gates on one population, after emit's gates on a strictly later population -- landing within 0.17% with the LATEST stopping point LOWEST is direct evidence that demand is fixed with respect to progress through those phases. A single completing run gives one number with no way to know whether it is a floor; only runs that stop at different places can answer that. The bound this supports is therefore over EVERYTHING UP TO AND INCLUDING EMIT'S GATES, and explicitly not over codegen.\n\nAN ATTRIBUTED OBSERVATION THIS SESSION DID NOT REPRODUCE, recorded because omitting it would leave the reader with three agreeing numbers and no reason to doubt them: warm-ant-908 reports a COMPLETING --target dag whole-corpus run (exit 0, zero blocking, wall 27:52) peaking at 15871708 kB = 15.14 GiB, on srv1, under a patched tree at be89e236c9. That is 2.7 GiB above every figure in this row. THAT DELTA IS THE DAG ROUTE'S EMISSION COST AND IS NOT EVIDENCE ABOUT THE RUST ROUTE, a qualifier warm-ant-908 supplied and this session verified against v1.compile emit_dag_artifact rather than accepting: the dag emitter serializes the WHOLE CORPUS INTO ONE STRING -- serialize_dag_nodes_table over the entire node table, then a single concat of nodes, modules, item_registry and diagnostics -- and returns files: [TextFile { path: \"dag-artifact.json\" }], ONE file for 4170 sources. So its emission half is dominated by one whole-corpus document resident at once. The rust emitter has no such object: it emits PER FILE (133 in the seed partition), so whether ITS emission half costs anything comparable is unmeasured, and the structural difference points the other way. What the attributed figure establishes is therefore the DIRECTION and not the MAGNITUDE -- a completing run's peak exceeds a refusing run's by enough to matter -- which is the only part the lower-bound claim below needs. IT IS ADOPTED, AND THE THRESHOLD IS DERIVED FROM IT RATHER THAN FROM THIS SESSION'S OWN THREE PEAKS. That reverses an earlier revision of this row, which declared 13 GiB and recorded this figure as a disclosure -- leaving every budget from 13 GiB to 15.14 GiB admitted with no evidence it can complete, which is the same fail-open class this module exists to close, one band narrower. Found by review 57202 on gunbc#9545, and the objection is correct: the argument for declining was PROVENANCE -- this session did not take the reading, and the tree behind it is not the tree these peaks measure -- and provenance is the wrong axis for a threshold whose failure is asymmetric. Over-refusal costs a run that would have fit; under-refusal admits a run that gets killed with no diagnostic. DESIGN section 6 names optimising the first at the expense of the second as the purity trap, and section 5 names the result as the arm that cannot fire for the population needing it.\n\nWHAT ADOPTION DOES AND DOES NOT ASSERT. It does NOT assert that this session reproduced 15871708 kB; the attribution above stands unchanged and the reading remains warm-ant-908's. It asserts only that a demand threshold must not sit BELOW the highest peak anyone has measured on this route, whoever measured it -- which is a statement about the DIRECTION a threshold may be wrong in, and needs no reproduction to be binding. The three peaks in this row keep their whole role: they are what establishes that demand is stable across gates, which is why a single completing figure can be trusted as a floor rather than as one draw from an unbounded distribution.\n\nTHE GRAIN RULE IS APPLIED UNCHANGED, AND IT LANDS ON THE KILL LINE BY ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN BY DESIGN. 15871708 kB = 15.14 GiB, rounded up to whole-gibibyte grain = 16 GiB = 17179869184, which is exactly gunbc.runner_slot_allocation's memory_max. That coincidence is stated rather than smoothed: adjusting the grain to make the number land somewhere more comfortable would be choosing a rule to produce a wanted answer, which is the reverse of an oracle. The rule was declared before this reading and is applied to it as declared.\n\nWHAT THAT COSTS, DELIBERATELY AND VISIBLY: the CI runner slot is now REFUSED. Its budget source is memory_high = 16106127360 (15 GiB), which is below this threshold, so whole_corpus_compile_admission refuses on that host and the witness asserting otherwise is inverted rather than edited to stay green. Read against gunbc.runner_slot_allocation's two lines, 15.14 GiB sits ABOVE the 15 GiB memory_high throttle and BELOW the 16 GiB memory_max kill line, so a completing run in a slot would degrade into swap rather than die -- the slot hosts it, slower. So this IS an over-refusal against survival, and it is the direction chosen: the budget a host REPORTS is the budget it has agreed to give, and admitting a run on the grounds that it will breach that budget and survive on swap is admitting a known breach. An admission arm that reasons about what a host will tolerate past its stated line is not fail-closed, whatever its outcome. Nothing in CI runs a whole-corpus compile today, so the refusal blocks no live route; if one is ever wanted there, the answer is a slot provisioned above the demand, not a threshold lowered to fit the slot.\n\nNO INSTRUMENT PRODUCES ANY FIGURE IN THIS ROW, AND THE ROW SAYS SO RATHER THAN LEAVING A READER TO ASSUME ONE EXISTS. Every number above was taken by driving `gunbc compile` BY HAND on srv1 under /usr/bin/time -v. There is no `.dag` entry point that re-derives them, so a reader who wants to check this figure cannot run something -- they must reproduce the procedure. That is precisely the state DESIGN's measurement-bankruptcy ruling names: if a measurement is worth re-deriving it is worth an entry point, and a figure copied into prose is debt whether or not a producer exists for it.\n\nWHY THE FIGURES ARE HERE ANYWAY, stated as a declared exception rather than an oversight: a DEMAND THRESHOLD WITH NO RECEIPT CANNOT BE CHALLENGED OR RE-MEASURED. The re-measure trigger below is defined by comparison against these readings, so deleting them would leave a merge-blocking literal whose only defence is that someone once measured something -- the unfalsifiable form DESIGN section 5 rejects. The exception covers the readings this row's own trigger consumes and NOTHING ELSE.\n\nNEXT-RUNG TRIGGER FOR THIS PARAGRAPH SPECIFICALLY: a `.dag` entry point that takes a scope and a target, runs the compile under a resident-set observation, and returns exit status and peak in ONE carrier -- at which point these figures are replaced by its name and the exception retires. `tools.emission_entry_instrument` is the nearest existing shape and already binds a scope, a target and both binary digests; what it does not carry is the resident-set observation, which is the whole subject here.\n\nRE-MEASURE TRIGGER, NARROWED BECAUSE THE PROXY IS GONE: a dated uncensored whole-corpus peak on the compile route, taken with a binary built from the subject sha, that EXCEEDS this figure. A demand figure does not shrink with corpus growth, so a lower later reading is not a reason to lower this one. THE VINTAGE CLAUSE IS PART OF THE TRIGGER AND NOT DECORATION: the first attempt at this measurement used a binary 91 stage0 Rust files behind its subject and reported a DIFFERENT BLOCKING POPULATION — one diagnostic of an entirely different class — so a peak taken under a mismatched compiler measures neither the tree nor the compiler anyone runs. Deliberately NOT re-derived by scaling this peak by a module count; the relationship between corpus size and peak resident set is still unmeasured, and a fabricated scaling law would be a heuristic standing where a measurement was available (DESIGN section 4)."

// RUNG: MITIGATABLE, and saying so is load-bearing because the neighbouring claim would be
// inflation. What this admission does is convert a SILENT SIGKILL — which sits below the ladder
// entirely, being a fabricated zero rather than any rung of it — into a typed, located, exit-1
// refusal that names the two disagreeing quantities, the budget, its source, and a remedy. That is
// containment of harm at a boundary, not prevention of the state.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM: an admitted budget is NOT certified sufficient. The threshold is a peak
// taken on the compile route itself rather than on a proxy route -- so the 'different route' half
// of the qualification this clause used to carry is retired. TWO HALVES SURVIVE AND THE SECOND WAS
// ADDED 2026-08-28 WITH THE COMPLETING-RUN ADOPTION. First, it is ONE tree's peak, and a demand
// figure does not shrink with corpus growth, so admission means 'not provably doomed at the tree
// that was measured', never 'will fit'. Second, the adopted reading was taken by another session on
// a DIFFERENT tree from the receipts in this module -- deliberately, because a threshold must not
// sit below the highest peak anyone has measured, but it means the 'at a named sha with a binary
// built from it' guarantee holds of the three refusing peaks and NOT of the declared figure. That
// is a real weakening of the threshold's provenance, accepted in exchange for closing the band it
// was leaving admitted, and it is stated here rather than left for a reader to reconstruct from the
// demand note. A run that clears this and dies anyway is a re-measure trigger, not a defect in the
// refusal.
//
// NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a per-run resident-set prediction derived from the compile's own declared
// inputs rather than a fleet-wide constant, at which point the admission becomes a real fit
// predicate and can climb toward mechanical prevention. Until then the class is declared here
// rather than left to be rediscovered.

// SCOPED COMPILES REACH NO ADMISSION ARM AT ALL, and that placement is unchanged — but THE
// ASYMMETRY THIS NOTE USED TO REST ON HAS CLOSED, and saying so is the point of this paragraph. It
// read that a scoped compile 'was measured to fit on the very runner that killed the whole-corpus
// run'. Re-measured 2026-08-28 (session clever-tern-899, srv1, same tree and binary as
// whole_corpus_compile_measured_demand_note): the scoped compile of src/v2/std/node.dag returns
// EXIT=0 with one file emitted and peaks at 5768388 kB — about 5.5 GiB, ABOVE the roughly 4.9 GiB
// that runner reported. So it no longer fits there either, and a reader taking the old sentence
// would be reassured about a fit that does not exist.
//
// WHAT SURVIVES THE CORRECTION, because the note's actual argument never depended on that fit: a
// scoped compile's resident working set is one module's reference-derived closure and not the whole
// tree, so a whole-tree demand figure is the wrong DENOMINATOR for it. That is a statement about
// grain, not about any particular runner, and it is why applying this threshold to scoped runs
// would refuse runs that demonstrably succeed. The gap between 5.5 GiB scoped and 12.4 GiB
// whole-corpus is the measured size of that grain difference.
//
// An unasked question, not an all-clear: a scoped compile is not admitted by this module, it is
// outside its subject. If scoped compiles later need their own bound it will be a different
// measurement with a different demand figure, never this one reused at a discount.

// The demand figure: the highest available COMPLETING-run peak on the compile route (15871708 kB,
// --target dag), rounded up to whole-gibibyte grain. This session's own three peaks are all lower
// AND all from REFUSING runs, so declaring one of them would leave the band between them and the
// completing evidence admitted without evidence it can complete. See
// whole_corpus_compile_measured_demand_note for why the higher figure is adopted despite being
// attributed rather than reproduced here, the grain argument, and the re-measure trigger.
fn whole_corpus_compile_measured_peak_demand() -> ByteSize {
  byte_size(17179869184)
}

type WholeCorpusCompileAdmission
  = WholeCorpusCompileAdmitted { budget: ByteSize, required: ByteSize }
  | WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetBelowMeasuredDemand {
      budget: ByteSize,
      required: ByteSize,
      source: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetUnreadable { reason: NonEmptyStr }

fn whole_corpus_compile_admission_at_budget(
  bytes: ByteSize,
  source: HostBudgetSource
) -> WholeCorpusCompileAdmission {
  if byte_size_count(b: bytes) < byte_size_count(b: whole_corpus_compile_measured_peak_demand()) {
    WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetBelowMeasuredDemand {
      budget: bytes,
      required: whole_corpus_compile_measured_peak_demand(),
      source: host_budget_source_label(s: source),
    }
  } else {
    WholeCorpusCompileAdmitted {
      budget: bytes,
      required: whole_corpus_compile_measured_peak_demand(),
    }
  }
}

fn whole_corpus_compile_admission(budget: HostBudgetResolution) -> WholeCorpusCompileAdmission {
  match budget {
    HostBudgetUnreadable { reason: r } => WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetUnreadable { reason: r }
    HostBudgetDeclaredUnverified { requested_bytes: _, reason: r } => WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetUnreadable { reason: r }
    HostBudgetResolved {
      effective_bytes: b,
      requested_bytes: _,
      observation: HostBudgetObserved { source: s, bytes: _ }
    } => whole_corpus_compile_admission_at_budget(bytes: b, source: s)
  }
}

fn whole_corpus_compile_admits(a: WholeCorpusCompileAdmission) -> Bool {
  match a {
    WholeCorpusCompileAdmitted { budget: _, required: _ } => true
    WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetBelowMeasuredDemand { budget: _, required: _, source: _ } => false
    WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetUnreadable { reason: _ } => false
  }
}

// The budget the BuildBuddy runner actually answered with, recovered exactly from the
// cap=1675 line that run printed (see whole_corpus_compile_observed_kill_receipt_note): the
// low end of the [5269094400, 5272240128) interval that division admits. Every value in the
// interval produces the same cap and the same verdict here.
data whole_corpus_compile_observed_runner_budget_lower_bound: ByteSize = byte_size(5269094400)

data whole_corpus_compile_seed_mirror_disposition: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.whole_corpus_compile_admission",
    decl_name: "whole_corpus_compile_admission",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

data whole_corpus_compile_seed_mirror_note: String = "v1 HAND-REALIZES THIS, FOR THE SAME BOOTSTRAP REASON gunbc.host_budget_source host_budget_source_seed_mirror_note and gunbc.typed_module_cache_capacity typed_module_cache_capacity_seed_mirror_note give, and no other: the decision runs BEFORE any .dag value could exist, because it is the decision about whether resolving the corpus may begin at all. It cannot be a value obtained by resolving the corpus. That is a bootstrap constraint, not ordinary seed-shrink debt, and conflating the two would misprice the work. The mirror is v1_compiler.memory_governor DECLARED_WHOLE_CORPUS_COMPILE_MEASURED_DEMAND_BYTES and whole_corpus_compile_admission over the same budget authority read_host_budget_bytes, joined in v1_compiler.cli_run `compile_emission`, on that transaction's PrimaryRoot arm, before the module index is built and deliberately not asked of Entry.\n\nTHIS SENTENCE NAMED main.rs UNTIL 2026-08-28 AND THE JOIN IS NOT THERE, which is worth recording because of what the stale citation cost a reader. A reviewer searching main.rs for the call site found none, and concluded from its absence that NO PRODUCTION CALLER EXISTS — that the arm is dormant and the refusal has never executed. That inference was wrong and the citation is why it was available. Refuted by EXECUTION on 2026-08-28: a whole-root compile under GUNBC_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES=5368709120 exits 1 with WholeCorpusCompileBudgetBelowMeasuredDemand naming both quantities, the source and the remedy, while a scoped --entry compile under the SAME budget is not refused by this arm and proceeds to the frontend. Two arms, one varied input, opposite outcomes: the arm is wired and the Entry asymmetry is real. A grep of the file a note names is not a census of callers, and the remedy for that is to name the symbol that holds the call rather than the file someone remembers it in (DESIGN section 3).\n\nTHIS ROW DECLARES NO DISSOLUTION OF ITS OWN, AND THAT IS THE POINT. typed_module_cache_capacity_seed_mirror_note already specifies the exact mechanism that retires this constant: one lens reading the seed's constants against the rows that own them, which it says would cover gunbc.typed_module_cache_capacity, gunbc.host_budget_source and DECLARED_RUNNER_SLOT_MEMORY_HIGH_BYTES together. DECLARED_WHOLE_CORPUS_COMPILE_MEASURED_DEMAND_BYTES lives in the same file as that last one and is retired by the same lens on the same day. Authoring a fourth locally-argued dissolution beside three identical ones is the forked-logic tell DESIGN section 3 names — the merge is already specified, so this row joins it rather than restating it. The wider dissolution is the same one those notes carry: the emit path bakes these constants into the seed and the seed stops composing its own arithmetic.\n\nTHE TRIGGER WAS INERT WHEN THIS ROW WAS WRITTEN AND IS BEING BUILT NOW; a reader must not infer a guard from the citation above without reading this paragraph. Measured at 517fdac4dd by stern-heron-695 and re-checked here: dag/test/claim/typed_module_cache_capacity_witness_test.dag carried eight test fns and referenced no .rs path, no stage0 projection, and not the per-entry byte literal — it checked the model against itself. memory_governor.rs is a hand_maintained_stage0_filenames() member and so is excluded from the derived generated population (gunbc.stage0_rust_source_lifecycle_scaffold derived_generated_stage0_repo_paths), confirming it is hand-maintained source rather than a generated projection, so this constant is knowingly-added seed source. The cost of the gap was not hypothetical: DECLARED_RUNNER_SLOT_MEMORY_HIGH_BYTES sat eleven days at a value its authority had superseded, under a note that read as though the drift were guarded.\n\nWHAT IS LANDING, AND WHAT IT WILL AND WILL NOT BUY. gunbc#8638 builds that lens (test.claim.seed_mirror_constant_lens_witness_test), joining seed constants to their authority rows and REFUSING an uncited or unparseable one rather than skipping it. Its in-scope set is an ENUMERATED list, not a set derived from the seed file, so a constant that is not on it — including this one at the time of writing — is neither checked nor refused; joining is a row someone adds, which is why the seed constant here is written as a canonical decimal literal and names its authority symbol in a doc comment, so that adding the row is a one-liner. Its ENROLLMENT MARKER is deliberately withheld: that lens's backward arm requires marker occurrences per seed file to equal roster rows homed in that file, so a marked constant with no row reds main for however long the two land apart. Row and marker land together, in whichever PR is second. When it lands the class becomes MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE and not structural: the invalid state stays writable and safety depends on the lens executing and staying enrolled. THIS PARAGRAPH IS REWRITTEN, NOT AMENDED, WHEN THE SIXTH ROW LANDS."

